  "notes": [
    {
      "title": "Use an explicit class-distribution example",
      "text": "Assume a $300,000 benefit, two child branches, and a designation preserving each child’s branch per stirpes. One child is alive; the other died earlier leaving two living children. The living child receives $150,000; each grandchild in the other branch receives $75,000. Under an expressly stated surviving-children-only per-capita designation, the one surviving child would instead receive $300,000.",
      "example": "The result comes from the specified designation. Do not assume all uses of class language have identical drafting.",
      "topics": [
        "5.2.01"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Contingent does not mean simultaneous half-owner",
      "text": "A contingent beneficiary is next in the stated succession when the primary cannot take. If the sole eligible primary takes the full benefit under the designation, the contingent does not automatically receive half. Other share instructions, survival terms and legal restrictions can affect a real claim.",
      "example": "A surviving eligible primary with a 100% designation ordinarily receives that stated share; the contingent is the backup.",
      "topics": [
        "5.2.05"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Minor, trust and estate designations need different administration",
      "text": "A minor’s entitlement and the lawful person who receives or manages funds are separate issues. A properly arranged trust identifies a trustee and management terms. Naming the estate sends the benefit into estate administration; a direct eligible named beneficiary generally receives proceeds outside probate. Neither probate treatment nor a trust label alone guarantees a particular tax result.",
      "example": "A parent should coordinate the designation with the chosen legal arrangement rather than assume a minor can immediately sign every required release.",
      "topics": [
        "5.2.02",
        "5.2.03",
        "5.2.04"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Divorce does not supply one answer for every document",
      "text": "MIA Bulletin 26-22, dated September 10, 2026, describes a Maryland-sitused life policy paying the unchanged former-spouse beneficiary despite a divorce in Pennsylvania. The governing policy and law mattered. Maryland Estates and Trusts §4-105 separately revokes specified spousal will provisions following absolute divorce or annulment unless the will or decree provides otherwise. Do not transfer that will rule to every life designation.",
      "example": "A question naming a federal plan, a specific agreement or an explicit contract provision requires examining those additional facts.",
      "topics": [
        "5.2.07"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Maryland simultaneous-death law changes October 1, 2026",
      "text": "As of September 12, 2026, Courts and Judicial Proceedings §10-804 provides that when there is insufficient evidence the insured and beneficiary died other than simultaneously, life proceeds are distributed as though the insured survived the beneficiary; §10-805 recognizes a contrary contract distribution. Chapter 509 takes effect October 1, 2026 and generally introduces a 120-hour survival requirement with clear-and-convincing-evidence and specified exceptions, including applicable common-disaster language or an express different survival period.",
      "example": "Use the event date and governing contract. The video’s stated 30-day contract scenario is not a claim that Maryland always requires 30 days or always requires 120 hours.",
      "topics": [
        "5.2.08"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "The facility-of-payment statute has specific conditions",
      "text": "Under §16-212(b), a permitted individual-life clause can address the named beneficiary’s failure to claim or surrender proof within a policy period of at least 30 days after death, an estate beneficiary, a minor, a beneficiary who predeceased the insured, or one unable legally to give a valid release. Permitted recipients include the estate, listed relatives or a person equitably entitled under the statute. It is a policy permission subject to conditions, not an unrestricted command to pay any relative.",
      "example": "A valid adult primary beneficiary promptly submitting the required claim does not lose priority simply because another relative prefers payment.",
      "topics": [
        "5.2.09"
      ]
    }
  ],
